# K9 — dense f64 matmul, NAIVE path: the triple loop written in comprehensions, with no
# tensor and no GEMM. This is the file to compare against naive C/Rust/Go: same
# arithmetic, same ijk order, same materialized row-major A and B, same strided B[k*n+j]
# read in the inner loop, and single-threaded like them (measured ~108% CPU, one core).
#
# THE INNER LOOP IS A DIRECT REDUCE, and that is the FAITHFUL port, not a favour to
# Helix: C/Rust/Go accumulate the inner product into a scalar register and allocate
# nothing, so `(0..n).reduce(0.0, (s, k) => s + a[i*n+k] * b[k*n+j])` is the literal
# transcription. The alternative spelling `(0..n).map(k => ...).reduce(...)` materializes
# an n-element array for EVERY (i,j) pair — 262,144 temporary arrays at n=512 that no
# sibling allocates — so it measures an allocation C never performs. That spelling is
# kept as k9_matmul_naive_maptemp.helix rather than deleted, because it is natural Helix
# and its cost is a real, currently-unfixed gap (see below); it is reported beside this
# one instead of standing in for it.
#
# MEASURED, min of 3 at n=512 on an idle 6-core box (2026-07-17):
#
#   C -O3 -march=native ............ 0.37s
#   Helix, this file ............... 0.45s   <- 1.2x C, single-threaded, same algorithm
#   Helix, _maptemp spelling ....... ~25s    <- 55x slower: the map-side gap, below
#
# HISTORY — WHY THIS FILE'S HEADER ONCE SAID 69x. Until the affine-index work landed,
# NEITHER spelling reached the JIT and this kernel ran entirely on the bytecode VM at
# ~25s (69x C). The blocker was never "nested comprehensions can't JIT" — a nested
# map-of-reduce with a BARE counter index (`a[k]`) always compiled fine. It was that the
# index `a[i*n+k]` is an `Expr::Binary`, and the reduce collector only admitted
# `arr[counter]` and `arr[scalar]`. Admitting an AFFINE index (`base + coef*counter`,
# with base/coef loop-invariant and the VM proving the two endpoints in bounds — see
# `IndexBound::Affine`) took this file from ~24s to 0.45s, a 53x change with the anchor
# unmoved. Do not resurrect the old claim: measure it.
#
# THE REMAINING GAP (real, and it is Helix's own): the map kernel's eligibility has no
# `Expr::Index` arm, so `map(k => a[...])` still falls to the VM — which is the whole of
# the _maptemp variant's 55x. Fixing that is the map-side twin of this file's fix.
#
#   A[i][j] = ((i*j) % 100) * 0.5      B[i][j] = ((i+j) % 100) * 0.25
#   anchor  = C[0][0] + C[n-1][n-1] + C[n/2][n/2]   printed as C's %.6f
#
# A and B are materialized as flat row-major arrays and INDEXED (not recomputed) in the
# inner loop, exactly as the C/Rust/Go siblings do. Recomputing ((i*k)%100)*0.5 on the
# fly would be a different benchmark (it skips the cache-missing strided load that
# dominates a naive ijk matmul), so it is not done here.
#
# The anchor is bit-exact for any accumulation order; see k8_matmul.helix for why.
#
# Helix has no argv, so n comes from stdin — `??` supplies the default at EOF. ALWAYS
# redirect stdin, or a bare `helix run` blocks forever waiting on a line:
#
#   helix run k9_matmul_naive.helix < /dev/null   # default n=512
#   echo 64 | helix run k9_matmul_naive.helix     # n=64, for anchor verification
n = read_int() ?? 512

# floor division — Helix's `/` is float division, so integer division is explicit.
fn idiv(x, m) = round((x - (x % m)) / m)

# flat row-major A and B: index t = row*n + col
a = (0..(n * n)).map(t => ((idiv(t, n) * (t % n)) % 100) * 0.5)
b = (0..(n * n)).map(t => ((idiv(t, n) + (t % n)) % 100) * 0.25)

# The triple loop: for each (i, j), reduce over k into a scalar — C's `for k: sum +=
# a[i*n+k] * b[k*n+j]`, allocation for allocation. C is fully materialized (all n² cells
# are computed); the anchor reads three of them afterwards, so the O(n³) work is real and
# cannot be folded away.
c = (0..n).map(i =>
  (0..n).map(j =>
    (0..n).reduce(0.0, (s, k) => s + a[i * n + k] * b[k * n + j])))

h = idiv(n, 2)
v = c[0][0] + c[n - 1][n - 1] + c[h][h]

# emit as C's %.6f — see k8_matmul.helix for the rationale.
eighths = round(v * 8)
ip = idiv(eighths, 8)
micros = (eighths % 8) * 125000
frac = if micros == 0 then "000000" else "{micros}"
print("{ip}.{frac}")
